/**
 * Exporting Camel programs as ONNX models: `export_model` writes the model of a function applied
 * to an example argument, and `supported_operators` lists the operator URIs the backend can
 * lower. The module is the ONNX exporter, not a runtime: loading it and asking what it can lower
 * needs neither the ONNX runtime nor a GPU; only a run of `export_model` writes a file.
 */

// The declarations of the C++ module `modules/onnx` (design principles 6.4). This file is the one
// source of both operators' signatures; the C++ side keeps the kernels, the executor and the
// purity.
//
// `export_model`'s `with` parameters are declared once, with their defaults: a call gives them by
// name, leaves out the ones with a default, and the value it left out is this declaration's
// constant (design principles 4.4). `fn` and `example` are ordinary parameters, and the module's
// kernel is where the two facts a declaration cannot write are settled: `fn` is a function, and
// `example` is the argument of a one-parameter `fn` or one example per parameter.
//
// `supported_operators` only reads the lowering table compiled into the module: it is registered
// pure, and the declaration carries that (a declaration carries types, not effects), so a call
// may be folded, merged or run concurrently and its result may not be dropped.

module onnx

/// Lists the operator URIs the ONNX backend can lower at its default opset, in the order the
/// lowering table reports them. A capability report only: it reads the table compiled into the
/// module and never touches the file system or the ONNX runtime.
/// @pure
/// @example let uris = supported_operators()
export func supported_operators(): string[]

/// Writes the ONNX model of `fn` applied to arguments like `example` to the file `out`, at the
/// backend's default opset. `example` is one argument of a one-parameter `fn`, or a tuple with
/// one example per parameter; the function's graph is translated after the generic passes, and a
/// node the backend cannot lower is refused. `out`, `dynamic_axes` and `target` are `with`
/// parameters because they are runtime values: `dynamic_axes` names the axes of the tensor inputs
/// that are left unknown in the model (which is what lets one model run at several shapes), and
/// `target` names the runtime whose capability data decides whether a node this backend lowers is
/// one that runtime has (`'none'` switches the check off).
/// @effect writes a file and reads the function's graph
/// @fails error value `ExportUnsupported` when `fn` is not a function, when the graph cannot be translated (a call that remains, an operator without an ONNX lowering, a dtype or shape the exporter cannot name), or when the target runtime rejects a node; error value `IOError` when the model cannot be written
/// @example export_model<out: "/tmp/model.onnx">(fn, example)
/// @example export_model<out: "/tmp/model.onnx", dynamic_axes: [0]>(fn, example)
/// @example export_model<out: "/tmp/model.onnx", target: "onnxruntime-cpu">(fn, example)
/// @example-fragment the call needs a function value and an example of the arguments that function takes, which the description introduces (the exporter reads the function's graph, so no value a fragment can write stands for it)
#op('onnx:export_model')
with <out: string, dynamic_axes: int[] = [], target: string = 'onnxruntime-cpu'>
export func export_model::<F, E>(fn: F, example: E): void!

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Passes: functions the pipeline applies to the program
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Writes the model of the program's `main` (or of the one `export_model` call it makes) as an
/// ONNX file, translating the function `main` refers to itself. `out` overrides the file the
/// `out` argument of `export_model` names (then exactly one call is allowed) and names the file
/// when the program makes no request.
with <out: string = "">
export func export_model::<F>(f: F): F! when is_function::<F>()
